BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Some Like It Hot in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260702
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dda6b-59cc-be2f-090e-f2f741d4e082@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Pickle the Drag Queen\n\nThursday\, July 2\, 2026\n
 \nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 1m\n\n1959 | 
 121 min | USA | Black-and-White | English | Not Rated | 4K \nDIRECTED BY:
  Billy Wilder  \nWRITTEN BY: Billy Wilder\, I. A. L. Diamond \nWITH: Mar
 ilyn Monroe\, Tony Curtis\, Jack Lemmon\, George Raft\n\nType: Screenings\
 n\nMarilyn Monroe’s work in director Billy Wilder’s comedic classic is
  often cited as her best. The film was controversial for its racy humor an
 d story about two musicians (Tony Curtis and Jack Lemmon) who disguise the
 mselves as women to evade the Mafia. But of equal scandal were the Oscar-w
 inning costumes made by Orry-Kelly for Monroe’s Sugar Kane Kowalczyk. Bo
 th dresses are on display in the exhibit Marilyn Monroe: Hollywood Icon\, 
 on view through February 28\, 2027. Some Like It Hot was made during a yea
 r of dramatic highs and lows for Monroe\, with this film standing as a hig
 h point.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/some-like-it-hot-019d
 da6b-59cc-be2f-090e-f2f741d4e082
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tokyo Drifter
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260702
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd578-9525-65a2-2a4f-39807145503e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, July 2\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 22m\n\n1966 | 82 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese 
 | Not Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Seijun Suzuki.\nWRITTEN BY: Kohan Kawauchi
 \nWITH: Tetsuya Watari\, Chieko Matsubara\, Tamio Kawaji\, Ryuji Kita.\n\n
 Type: Screenings\n\nSeijun Suzuki takes a deep dive into a delirious world
  of yakuza\, centering around a killer named Tetsuya\, fresh out of prison
 \, who gets pulled into a gang war despite his wish to stay out of it. Suz
 uki’s visual orchestration is unhinged\, with its infinite flow of vivid
 \, stimulating colors clashing with the anticipated\, coherent style of th
 e narrative. It’s a liberated departure from the conventional structure 
 of storytelling—more similar to experimental film work. An unusual portr
 ayal of yakuza drama\, this thriller offers surreal\, sensational signifie
 rs through the exuberance of its highly stylized set design\, its bizarrel
 y choreographed blocking\, and offbeat pacing of its cuts.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/tokyo-drifter-019dd57
 8-9525-65a2-2a4f-39807145503e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sorcerer in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd579-9263-515c-5873-11100ff31dac@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 1m\n\n1977 | 121 min. | USA | Color | English/Spani
 sh/French/German | Rated R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: William Friedkin\nWRITTE
 N BY: Walon Green\nWITH: Roy Scheider\, Bruno Cremer\, Francisco Rabal\, A
 midou\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA thief (Roy Scheider) on the run after a hei
 st gone wrong find himself marooned in a South American village\, where hi
 s only chance to turn his life around is to join three equally desperate m
 en on a potentially suicidal mission: transporting two truckloads of nitro
 glycerin over treacherous mountain roads. For his first film after the one
 -two punch of The French Connection (1971) and The Exorcist (1973)\, direc
 tor William Friedkin remade Henri-Georges Clouzot’s classic 1953 thrille
 r The Wages of Fear (itself based on Georges Arnaud’s 1950 novel)\, stag
 ing breathtaking action scenes on remote locations. A box-office disappoin
 tment at the time of its release\, Sorcerer has since become one of Friedk
 in’s most enduring classics\, bolstered by its Oscar-nominated sound mix
 ing (Robert Knudson\, Robert J. Glass\, Richard Tyler\, Jean-Louis Ducarme
 ) and Tangerine Dream’s influential electronic score.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/sorcerer-019dd579-926
 3-515c-5873-11100ff31dac
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Whisper of the Heart in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd4f9-7849-b1b0-965c-537bb69da545@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 51m\n\n1995 | 111 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese w
 ith English subtitles | Rated G | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Yoshifumi Kondo \n
 WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki. \nWITH: Yoko Honna\, Issei Takahashi\, Takash
 i Tachibana\, Shigeru Muroi\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe first and only feat
 ure directed by veteran Studio Ghibli animator Yoshifumi Kondo\, from a sc
 ript by Hayao Miyazaki\, Whisper of the Heart is a wistful coming-of-age s
 aga. One day\, bookworm Shizuku finds a cat riding the same train as her. 
 She follows it to an antique shop\, where a peculiar statuette of a dapper
  feline named Baron Humbert von Gikkingen sends her imagination reeling. A
  tale of crushes\, daydreams\, and friendships\, Whisper of the Heart bear
 s the stamp of Studio Ghibli’s superior craftmanship.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/whisper-of-the-heart-
 019dd4f9-7849-b1b0-965c-537bb69da545
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Misfits in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dda6a-6f44-f26c-6d86-98e042029c2b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: actor Jack Huston\n\nFriday\, July 3\, 2026\n\nLoca
 tion: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n1961 | 125 mi
 n | USA | Black-and-White | English | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: J
 ohn Huston \nWRITTEN BY: Arthur Miller \nWITH: Clark Gable\, Marilyn Mon
 roe\, Montgomery Clift\, Thelma Ritter \nRestored by the Academy Film Arch
 ive and The Film Foundation. Restoration funding provided by the Hobson/Lu
 cas Family Foundation.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nPenned by Arthur Miller\, th
 is contemporary Western centers on the recently divorced Roslyn (Marilyn M
 onroe) and her relationship with an aging former cowboy\, Gay (Clark Gable
 )\, who now survives by rounding up wild mustangs to sell them to a slaugh
 terhouse. Considered a commercial failure at the time of its original rele
 ase\, the film has since been regarded as a classic by critics and audienc
 es\, perhaps notably because it was the final completed film of both Gable
  and Monroe\, and a fitting bookend to Monroe’s career—she credited Hu
 ston for her first big break in his The Asphalt Jungle (1950).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-misfits-019dda6a-
 6f44-f26c-6d86-98e042029c2b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Great Muppet Caper in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260704T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260704T121500
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019db104-9ede-2724-6ea8-30871fa1a1f9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 4\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 37m\n\n1981 | 97 min. | UK\, USA | Color
  | English | Rated G | 4K\nDIRECTED BY: Jim Henson \nWRITTEN BY: Tom Patc
 hett\, Jay Tarses\, Jerry Juhl\, Jack Rose \nWITH: Jim Henson\, Frank Oz\
 , Dave Goelz\, Jerry Nelson\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis second Muppets fea
 ture film casts Kermit the Frog\, Fozzie Bear\, and the Great Gonzo as int
 repid journalists on location in London to interview fashion icon Lady Hol
 iday about the recent theft of her jewels. Hired as Holiday’s secretary 
 is aspiring model Miss Piggy\, who poses as her boss when Kermit arrives. 
 Mistaken identities\, espionage\, and hijinks abound in this kooky escapad
 e that features cameos from comedy greats Charles Grodin\, John Cleese\, a
 nd international raconteur Peter Ustinov. The film received an Academy Awa
 rd nomination for Music (Original Song) for the delightful “The First Ti
 me It Happens.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-great-muppet-cape
 r-019db104-9ede-2724-6ea8-30871fa1a1f9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jaws in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260704T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260704T160000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019db107-26cd-12fd-87e7-a1ebc697e1fa@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 4\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n1975 | 124 min | USA | Color | S
 cope | English | Rated PG | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Steven Spielberg \nWRITT
 EN BY: Peter Benchley\, Carl Gottlieb \nWITH: Roy Scheider\, Robert Shaw\
 , Richard Dreyfuss\, Lorraine Gary\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSteven Spielberg
 ’s second feature as a director set the standard for edge-of-your-seat s
 uspense and quickly became a cultural phenomenon. When the seaside communi
 ty of Amity Island falls prey to a great white shark\, the chief of police
  (Roy Scheider)\, a young marine biologist (Richard Dreyfuss)\, and a griz
 zled shark hunter (Robert Shaw) embark on a desperate quest to destroy the
  beast before it strikes again. A box office record-breaker as well as a B
 est Picture nominee\, Jaws earned Oscars for film editing\, sound\, and Jo
 hn Williams’s thrilling score.\nOn the occasion of Jaws: The Exhibition
 —the largest mounted exhibition ever organized around Spielberg’s film
 \, experience the movie before diving into the collection. The museum’s 
 first of this scale focused exclusively on a single film\, the exhibition 
 features scene breakdowns\, interactive experiences\, behind-the-scenes st
 ories\, and more than 200 original objects\, many never before seen on pub
 lic display\, and follows the structure of the film\, taking visitors from
  the opening credits to its gripping conclusion. \nJaws: The Exhibition is
  curated by Jenny He\, senior exhibitions curator\, and Emily Rauber Rodri
 guez\, assistant curator\, with Alexandra James Salichs\, curatorial assis
 tant\, Academy Museum of Motion Pictures.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/jaws-019db107-26cd-12
 fd-87e7-a1ebc697e1fa
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Giant in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260705T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260705T160000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019d8807-8f35-89b4-4423-642b22f044e1@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 5\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of 
 Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 3h 18m\n\n1956 | 198 min. | USA | Color | Engl
 ish | Rated G | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: George Stevens \nWRITTEN BY: Fred Gu
 iol\, Ivan Moffat \nWITH: Elizabeth Taylor\, Rock Hudson\, James Dean\, Ca
 rroll Baker\n\nType: Screenings\n\nGeorge Stevens (A Place in the Sun\, 19
 51) earned his second Directing Oscar for this epic yet character-centered
  adaptation of Edna Ferber’s 1952 bestseller about a wealthy Texan (Best
  Actor nominee Rock Hudson)\, his blueblood bride (Elizabeth Taylor)\, and
  an ambitious ranch hand (Best Actor nominee James Dean\, in his final per
 formance). The film received 10 nominations including Best Picture\, Suppo
 rting Actress (Mercedes McCambridge) and for Dimitri Tiomkin’s original 
 score. For his only collaboration with Stevens\, the Ukraine-born Tiomkin 
 contributed authentic musical Americana\, including the memorable theme so
 ng “This Then Is Texas” (lyric by Paul Francis Webster).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/giant-019d8807-8f35-8
 9b4-4423-642b22f044e1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Private Property
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260705T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260705T150000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd57a-e7c4-4cd9-514c-266e25cf7f85@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 5\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 19m\n\n1960 | 79 min. | USA | Black-and-
 White | English | Not Rated | 4K DCP.\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Leslie Stevens
  \nWITH: Corey Allen\, Warren Oates\, Kate Manx\, Robert Wark \nPrint co
 urtesy of UCLA Film & Television Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA pair of
  drifters emerges from the Southern California ocean and hitches a ride to
  the well-appointed neighborhood of Beverly Hills\, where the twosome squa
 ts in an abandoned home. Next door\, a sex-deprived housewife struggles to
  resist the duo’s mysterious magnetism\, prompting psychological turmoil
 \, guilt\, and a host of quandaries about female agency. Made on a shoestr
 ing budget independent from a major studio\, Leslie Stevens’s disquietin
 g debut prompted the condemnation of the Catholic Legion of Decency\, whil
 e the MPAA denied the film its stamp of approval.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/private-property-019d
 d57a-e7c4-4cd9-514c-266e25cf7f85
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Three Days of the Condor
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260705T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260705T223000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd57b-abd2-99cd-0867-db4e92aa0321@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 5\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of 
 Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 57m\n\n1977 | 117 min. | USA | Color | Engl
 ish/Spanish/French/German | Rated R | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Sydney Pollack\nWR
 ITTEN BY: Lorenzo Semple Jr.\, David Rayfiel \nWITH: Robert Redford\, Faye
  Dunaway\, Cliff Robertson\, Max von Sydow\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJoe Turn
 er (Robert Redford) is part of a team analyzing printed media for the CIA\
 , but when his seemingly harmless job turns unexpectedly deadly\, he finds
  himself on the run from both his own government and forces unknown. The f
 ourth of seven collaborations between Robert Redford and director Sydney P
 ollack\, this classic of 70s paranoid cinema\, based on James Grady’s no
 vel Six Days of the Condor\, benefits from a stellar cast (including Faye 
 Dunaway as Redford’s unwitting hostage and Max von Sydow as a surprising
 ly reasonable assassin)\, Owen Roizman’s moody cinematography\, and the 
 Oscar-nominated editing of Fredric Steinkamp and Don Guidice.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/three-days-of-the-con
 dor-019dd57b-abd2-99cd-0867-db4e92aa0321
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Princess Mononoke in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260706T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260706T150000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd4fa-4622-28a9-6f79-b9f62112f72c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 6\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 13m\n\n1997 | 133 min. | Japan | Color |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Rated PG-13 | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY
 : Hayao Miyazaki \nWITH: Yoji Matsuda\, Yuriko Ishida\, Yuko Tanaka\, Kao
 ru Kobayashi\nNew subtitled print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive\, w
 ith special thanks to Studio Ghibli.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki
 ’s international breakthrough is an epic cinematic marvel. Infected by a
  deadly curse after killing a god\, young warrior Ashitaka heads west in s
 earch of a cure. He stumbles upon a bitter conflict between Lady Eboshi\, 
 the proud people of Iron Town\, and the enigmatic Princess Mononoke\, a gi
 rl raised by wolves and who will stop at nothing to prevent humans from de
 stroying her home\, and the forest spirits and animal gods who live there.
  Princess Mononoke resists an easy dichotomy of good versus evil and was t
 he first Miyazaki film that incorporated computer-generated imagery alongs
 ide traditional hand-drawn animation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/princess-mononoke-019
 dd4fa-4622-28a9-6f79-b9f62112f72c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:30th Anniversary of Bottle Rocket with Wes Anderson
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260706T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260706T223000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019de523-ce0e-d4b4-b0d9-578590072521@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: filmmaker Wes Anderson\, actor Luke Wilson\, produc
 er James L. Brooks\n\nMonday\, July 6\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy 
 Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 31m\n\n1996 | 91 min. | USA | Col
 or | English\, Spanish | Rated R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Wes Anderson \nWR
 ITTEN BY: Owen C. Wilson\, Wes Anderson  \nPRODUCED BY: Cynthia Hargrave\
 , Polly Platt \nWITH: Luke Wilson\, Owen C. Wilson\, Ned Dowd\, Shea Fowl
 er\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFor 30 years\, writer-director Wes Anderson has 
 cultivated a meticulous visual style that conjures layers of nostalgia whi
 le charting a unique and deeply personal cinematic language. With its shar
 p comedic beats and deadpan performances from brothers Luke and Owen Wilso
 n\, Anderson’s debut feature\, Bottle Rocket\, announces this first chap
 ter of his burgeoning sensibility—and reminds us he’s possessed his si
 ngular aesthetic all along. \nFilmed on location in and around Dallas\, th
 is playful caper\, co-written with co-star Owen Wilson and executive produ
 ced by James L. Brooks (Terms of Endearment\, 1983)\, blends the fleetfoot
 ed spirit of the French New Wave with the gritty\, real-world stakes of ea
 rly 1990s crime films for an indie gem that marks an auspicious start for 
 one of American cinema’s most consistently visionary talents.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/bottle-rocket-30th-an
 niversary-019de523-ce0e-d4b4-b0d9-578590072521
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Night of the Hunter in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260706T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260706T223000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd57c-a7a5-928e-8172-063ab7ffc190@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 6\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n1955 | 93 min. | USA. | Black-and
 -White | English | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Charles Laughton\nWRITTEN BY: James 
 Agee\nWITH: Robert Mitchum\, Shelley Winters\, Lillian Gish\, James Gleaso
 n\n\nType: Screenings\n\nPulitzer Prize winner James Agee adapted the 1953
  novel by Davis Grubb for this stylish and evocative suspense drama\, whic
 h was the only feature directed by Oscar -winning actor Charles Laughton (
 The Private Life of Henry VIII\, 1933). Robert Mitchum is Harry Powell\, a
  remorseless murderer who travels the countryside in the guise of a preach
 er.  He sets his sights on the widow (Shelley Winters) and children of a f
 ormer cellmate\, who may hold the key to a hidden fortune. Working in blac
 k-and-white with cinematographer Stanley Cortez (The Magnificent Ambersons
 \, 1942)\, Laughton creates a dream-like world of danger for the children\
 , anchored by the performance of screen legend Lillian Gish as their poten
 tial savior.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/night-of-the-hunter-0
 19dd57c-a7a5-928e-8172-063ab7ffc190
END:VEVENT
