BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Lovers on the Bridge (Les amants de Pont-Neuf) in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260708T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260708T223000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd52e-d672-d769-b172-eb2edced096f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Cinematographers Branch\n\nWednesday\, July 8\
 , 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntim
 e: 2h 5m\n\n1991 | 125 min. | France | Color | French | Rated R | 4K DCP \
 nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Leos Carax \nWITH: Juliette Binoche\, Denis Lavant\,
  Klaus-Michael Grüber\, Marion Stalens\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAn artist l
 osing her eyesight (Oscar-winner Juliette Binoche) and an unhoused circus 
 performer (Denis Lavant) form an unexpected connection on the streets of P
 aris in this visually extravagant romance from writer-director Leos Carax 
 (Holy Motors\, 2012). Carax worked on a modest scale on his first two film
 s\, Boy Meets Girl (1984) and Mauvais Sang (1986)\, but The Lovers on the 
 Bridge was an elaborate and infamously troubled project\, plagued by produ
 ction delays\, budget overruns\, and the necessity to recreate Paris’s P
 ont-Neuf on a massive set. The result received mixed reactions upon its re
 lease but has since been celebrated as one of the filmmaker’s most tower
 ing achievements\, enhanced greatly by the dazzling cinematography of Jean
 -Yves Escoffier (Good Will Hunting\, 1997).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-lovers-on-the-bri
 dge-019dd52e-d672-d769-b172-eb2edced096f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Princess Mononoke in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260709T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260709T150000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd50b-594c-9ff5-6a87-2437cb57b76a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, July 9\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 13m\n\n1997 | 133 min. | Japan | Color
  | Japanese with English subtitles | Rated PG-13 | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN 
 BY: Hayao Miyazaki \nWITH: Yoji Matsuda\, Yuriko Ishida\, Yuko Tanaka\, K
 aoru Kobayashi\nNew subtitled print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive\,
  with special thanks to Studio Ghibli.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyaza
 ki’s international breakthrough is an epic cinematic marvel. Infected by
  a deadly curse after killing a god\, young warrior Ashitaka heads west in
  search of a cure. He stumbles upon a bitter conflict between Lady Eboshi\
 , the proud people of Iron Town\, and the enigmatic Princess Mononoke\, a 
 girl raised by wolves and who will stop at nothing to prevent humans from 
 destroying her home\, and the forest spirits and animal gods who live ther
 e. Princess Mononoke resists an easy dichotomy of good versus evil and was
  the first Miyazaki film that incorporated computer-generated imagery alon
 gside traditional hand-drawn animation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/princess-mononoke-019
 dd50b-594c-9ff5-6a87-2437cb57b76a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How Does the Silver Screen Shape the Golden State? Featuring Frida
 y
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260710T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260710T210000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019e8942-5046-8528-2ad4-4cdd6167d315@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations,Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Academy Museum and Zócalo Public Square explore the industry\
 , imagination\, and influence of California film featuring a screening of 
 Friday (1995)\n\nFriday\, July 10\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 37m\n\n1995 | 97 min. | USA | Color |
  English | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: F. Gary Gray \nWRITTEN BY: Ice C
 ube\, DJ Pooh \nWITH: Ice Cube\, Chris Tucker\, Nia Long\, John Witherspoo
 n \nSchedule\n7–7:40pm | Pre-screening conversation | Ted Mann Theater\
 n7:40–9pm | Friday screening | Ted Mann Theater\n9:15–9:45pm | Post-sc
 reening mixer | TMT Lobby\n\nType: Conversations\, Screenings\n\nThis scre
 ening of Friday (1995) and conversation is co-presented by the Academy Mus
 eum and Zócalo Public Square\, with special guests: Darnell Hunt\, Execut
 ive Vice Chancellor and Provost\, UCLA\, and Anikah McLaren\, Head of Film
 \, Higher Ground. Moderated by Oscar Garza\, Director\, Specialized Journa
 lism in Arts & Culture Program\, USC.\nIt's part of Zócalo's California 1
 75 — What Connects California? series\, a suite of free Zócalo events a
 nd journalism bringing together leaders and thinkers from all walks of lif
 e to envision California's next 175 years.\nCinema has always held a speci
 al place for the California imagination\, often recreating its cities\, be
 aches\, and wilderness through a lens of riches\, romance\, and reinventio
 n—the California Dream. \nBut does that dream still exist? As Hollywood 
 faces new challenges from streaming\, AI\, and industry flight\, is the Ca
 lifornia imagination changing? How about the way the world sees the Golden
  State? Can it remain the epicenter of cinematic storytelling through the 
 rest of the 21st century? \nAbout the filmIt’s Friday\, and recently une
 mployed Craig (Ice Cube) has no job. With nothing to do\, he links up with
  his stoner friend Smokey (Chris Tucker) in their South Central Los Angele
 s neighborhood. What starts as a simple day spirals into a raucous adventu
 re as the two seek to settle a debt with drug dealer Big Worm (Faizon Love
 )\; navigate eccentric locals\; and dodge local bully Deebo (Tiny “Zeus
 ” Lister Jr.). \nCo-written by Ice Cube and filmed on location\, Friday 
 paints a loving tribute to the South LA area where he grew up\, drawing on
  personal experiences to craft a light-hearted tale filled with memorable 
 characters\, quotable lines\, and stories of Southland life that have reso
 nated worldwide. \nAbout our co-hostsFounded in Los Angeles in 2003\, Zóc
 alo Public Square is a unit of ASU Media Enterprise and syndicates journal
 ism to media outlets worldwide. It has hosted more than 750 free events in
  cities including New York\, Washington D.C.\, Chicago\, Houston\, San Fra
 ncisco\, Shanghai\, Mexico City\, London\, and Berlin. To learn more\, vis
 it zocalopublicsquare.org\, or like and follow on Instagram\, Facebook\, X
 \, YouTube\, and LinkedIn.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/how-does-the-silver-s
 creen-shape-the-golden-state-featuring-friday-1995-019e8942-5046-8528-2ad4
 -4cdd6167d315
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Blow Out in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260710T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260710T223000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd57d-7000-1a17-a7ea-f705d9ff2f9c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 10\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1981 | 108 min. | USA | Color | 
 Scope | English | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Brian De Palma \nW
 ITH: John Travolta\, Nancy Allen\, John Lithgow\, Dennis Franz\n\nType: Sc
 reenings\n\nBrian De Palma wrote and directed this mystery about a movie s
 ound technician (John Travolta) who inadvertently records the assassinatio
 n of a politician. Stylish and unexpectedly emotional\, Blow Out has becom
 e one of De Palma’s most acclaimed films over the last four decades\, an
 d his script cleverly incorporates filmmaking techniques into its storylin
 e. In collaboration with sound mixer Dick Vorisek and sound editor Dan Sab
 le\, as well as such De Palma regulars as cinematographer Vilmos Zsigmond\
 , film editor Paul Hirsch\, and composer Pino Donaggio\, the director crea
 tes a worthy successor to the great conspiracy thrillers of the 1970s.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/blow-out-019dd57d-700
 0-1a17-a7ea-f705d9ff2f9c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Clue
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T121500
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019db101-caa9-1616-9f8c-b6a9c029251c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 11\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 28m\n\n1985 | 88 min. | USA | Color | E
 nglish | Rated PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Jonathan Lynn \nWRITTEN BY: Jonat
 han Lynn \nWITH: Eileen Brennan\, Tim Curry\, Madeline Kahn\, Christopher
  Lloyd\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis darkly comedic adaptation of the popula
 r Parker Brothers board game takes a page from the spate of Agatha Christi
 e book-to-screen projects of the 1970s. Convening a murderer’s row of 
 ’80s comedic actors\, this cult-favorite whodunit may have been consider
 ed middling upon its release but soon found its adoring audience on home v
 ideo and through revival screenings. This DCP from Paramount features all 
 three endings shown sequentially\, as they were presented during televisio
 n broadcasts of the film and as featured on home video releases.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/clue-019db101-caa9-16
 16-9f8c-b6a9c029251c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Elevator to the Gallows
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T150000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd57e-11da-98b1-87d7-2de5abc187cd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 11\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n1958 | 93 min. | France | Blac
 k-and-White | French | Not Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Louis Malle\nWRITTEN 
 BY: Louis Malle\, Roger Nimier \nWITH: Jeanne Moreau\, Maurice Ronet\, Geo
 rges Poujouly\, Yori Bertin\n\nType: Screenings\n\nLouis Malle’s directo
 rial debut at age 24 is one of the most significant contributions to the F
 rench New Wave. The sensational and eloquently mesmerizing Jeanne Moreau s
 tars as Florence\, whose hopeless adoration of a secret lover\, Julien (Ma
 urice Ronet)\, jeopardizes her freedom. The couple plans a high-stakes mur
 der of Florence’s husband\, and Julien successfully executes the plot—
 until he gets trapped in an elevator. A series of ironic incidents ensue a
 gainst the backdrop of a modernized landscape of Paris\, while the cinemat
 ography is beautifully paired with a magical musical performance by the gr
 eat Miles Davis. It all comes together to amplify Florence’s deep emotio
 nal state in the deep night of a desolate\, apathetic city.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/elevator-to-the-gallo
 ws-019dd57e-11da-98b1-87d7-2de5abc187cd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Careful in 4K with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T213000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd537-2e34-6c78-8d7f-584841b78a06@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:US restoration premiere\nIn person: director Guy Maddin and fi
 lmmaker and author Sandi Tan\n\nSaturday\, July 11\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLo
 cation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n1992 | 100
  min. | Canada | Color | English | Not Rated | 4K DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Guy
  Maddin  \nWRITTEN BY: Guy Maddin\, George Toles \nSTORY BY: George Tole
 s \nWITH: Kyle McCulloch\, Gosia Dobrowolska\, Sarah Neville\, Brent Neal
 e\n\nType: Screenings\n\nGuy Maddin’s early masterpiece takes place in a
  19th-century Alpine village where the wary residents—adult\, child and 
 animal!—must speak softly and tread lightly lest they cause an avalanche
 . But sexual frenzies teem in this world of repression\, setting off inces
 tuous love triangles and quadrangles with deadly consequences. Bathed in l
 urid\, luminescent tints\, Careful resembles a vintage melodrama from anot
 her planet—something that could only emerge from the singular mind of Ma
 ddin. —Zeitgeist Films
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/careful-with-guy-madd
 in-019dd537-2e34-6c78-8d7f-584841b78a06
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Magnificent Seven in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260712T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260712T160000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019d8807-479d-3bdf-e11e-5f3a97e1777d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 12\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of
  Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 8m\n\n1960 | 128 min. | USA | Color | Scop
 e | English\, Spanish | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: John Sturges \nWRITTEN BY: W
 illiam Roberts \nWITH: Yul Brynner\, Eli Wallach\, Steve McQueen\, Horst B
 uchholz\n\nType: Screenings\n\nYul Brynner plays a gunslinger leading a se
 ptet of strangers to defend a Mexican village against marauders in directo
 r John Sturges’ classic Western remake of Akira Kurosawa’s Seven Samur
 ai—a remake that itself inspired three sequels\, a TV series\, and its o
 wn 2016 remake. Brynner’s fellow gunslingers include then-rising stars S
 teve McQueen\, Charles Bronson and James Coburn. Elmer Bernstein received 
 his second Oscar nomination for his rousing score\, whose classic main the
 me became arguably his most popular and widely recognized melody.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-magnificent-seven
 -019d8807-479d-3bdf-e11e-5f3a97e1777d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Green Fog with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260712T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260712T213000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd538-3ef3-53b8-2fc6-aaf84e3fc5dd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 12\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 3m\n\n2017 | 63 min. | USA | Color | En
 glish | Not Rated | DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Guy Maddin\, Evan Johnson\, Galen
  Johnson \nWITH: Rock Hudson\, Karl Malden\, Chuck Norris\, John Saxon (a
 rchive footage)\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe Green Fog is a new version\, in
  a wonderfully oblique fashion\, of Hitchcock’s Vertigo\; it could also 
 have been named San Francisco Plays Itself or\, to borrow a title from Pet
 er Greenaway\, Vertical Features Remake. Hitchcock’s film is one of the 
 most vertically obsessed films ever\, and The Green Fog follows suit\, but
  since this is a work co-directed by Guy Maddin\, you can imagine that it
 ’s also obsessive on many other levels that Hitchcock could never have d
 reamed of. The Green Fog tells a story of sorts\, essentially reproducing 
 the shape of Vertigo—with some room for facetiously digressive play—us
 ing clips from assorted films and TV series\, all shot on location in San 
 Francisco\, to stand in for moments of Hitchcock’s film. —Jonathan Rom
 ney\, Film Comment
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-green-fog-with-gu
 y-maddin-019dd538-3ef3-53b8-2fc6-aaf84e3fc5dd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Winnipeg in 35mm with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260712T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260712T213000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd539-00fa-31a2-67b6-c57af87fcdb1@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 12\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 20m\n\n2007 | 80 min. | Canada | Black-
 and-White\, Color | English | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Guy Maddin \
 nWRITTEN BY: Guy Maddin\, George Toles \nWITH: Ann Savage\, Louis Negin\,
  Amy Stewart\, Darcy Fehr\n\nType: Screenings\n\nCommissioned as a documen
 tary about the director’s prairie hometown\, My Winnipeg proceeds from t
 he simple premise that a city is nothing without its ghosts: the mass of m
 emories\, legends and fictions that accumulate in any place inhabited by h
 uman beings. My Winnipeg is a film about the dream life of a city\, a para
 -documentary about a parallel\, phantasmagorical\, invisible Winnipeg\; it
  is a séance\, a channeling of spirits through the medium of cinema\, an 
 exorcism of personal and collective history\, as well as a memoir of one s
 oul’s desperate final attempt at an escape from the city and its ghosts.
  —Tyler Prozeniuk\, POV Magazine
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/my-winnipeg-with-guy-
 maddin-019dd539-00fa-31a2-67b6-c57af87fcdb1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Neighbors the Yamadas
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260713T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260713T150000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd511-a205-b46b-3a95-3db863a98edd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 13\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 44m\n\n1999 | 104 min. | Japan | Color 
 | Japanese with English subtitles | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: I
 sao Takahata \nWITH: Yukiji Asaoka\, Toru Masuoka\, Masako Araki\, Hayato
  Isohata\n\nType: Screenings\n\nIsao Takahata’s adaptation of Hisaichi I
 shii’s beloved slice-of-life manga departs from Studio Ghibli’s tradit
 ional anime style and instead uses a roughly sketched comic-strip look. Aw
 ash in pastel watercolors\, My Neighbors the Yamadas delights in the every
 day misadventures of its titular family—dad\, mom\, older brother\, litt
 le sister\, granny\, and Pochi the dog. Punctuated by daydreams and fantas
 ies\, Takahata’s film is a heart-warming and honest look at modern famil
 y life. Along with its typical animation style\, Ghibli forgoes its usual 
 long\, orchestral music in favor of short piano themes to match its episod
 ic style\, making the film a standout in the studio’s filmography.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/my-neighbors-the-yama
 tas-019dd511-a205-b46b-3a95-3db863a98edd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Saddest Music in the World with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260713T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260713T213000
DTSTAMP:20260704T101017Z
UID:019dd53b-afab-95c2-4ae6-6e0f0c70c508@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 13\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n2003 | 100 min. | Canada | Color
 \, Black-and-White | English\, Spanish | Rated R | DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Gu
 y Maddin.  \nWRITTEN BY: Kazuo Ishiguro\, George Toles\, Guy Maddin.  \n
 WITH: Mark McKinney\, Isabella Rossellini\, Maria de Medeiros\, David Fox\
 n\nType: Screenings\n\nGuy Maddin’s The Saddest Music in the World exist
 s in a time and place we have never seen before\, although it claims to be
  set in Winnipeg in 1933. The city\, we learn\, has been chosen by the Lon
 don Times\, for the fourth year in a row\, as “the world capital of sorr
 ow.” Here Lady Port-Huntley (Isabella Rossellini) has summoned entries f
 or a contest which will award $25\,000 “in Depression Era dollars” to 
 the performer of the saddest music. The Canadian filmmaker has devised a s
 tyle that evokes old films from an alternate timeline\; The Saddest Music 
 is not silent and not entirely in black-and-white\, but it looks like a lo
 ng-lost classic from decades ago\, grainy and sometimes faded\; he shoots 
 on 8mm film and video and blows it up to look like a memory from cinema’
 s distant past…You have never seen a film like this before\, unless you 
 have seen other films by Guy Maddin.  —Roger Ebert\, Chicago Sun-Times
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-saddest-music-in-
 the-world-with-guy-maddin-019dd53b-afab-95c2-4ae6-6e0f0c70c508
END:VEVENT
